[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Genoud et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5fcd98fb07a48b9ae4a670d6f5ae843ecfdbcd8b)
- venue: Applied physics B
- citation count: 71
[method]
The paper used a controlled optical backscattering system to measure mosquito wing-beat frequency and separate body from wing contributions. A continuous-wave 1320 nm infrared laser diode with 3.6 W power was collimated to a 2 cm full-width at half-maximum beam and transmitted through a 1.25 m long, 12.5 cm diameter Plexiglas tube located 3 m from the output mirror. The beam entered through a borosilicate glass window tilted 5 degrees downward and was stopped by an infrared beam stop. Mosquitoes were introduced into the tube through small apertures. A 7 cm diameter off-axis parabolic gold mirror, set coaxially with the laser beam, collected backscattered light and focused it through a 9.8 mm aperture onto a 3.14 mm squared InGaAs amplified photodetector with 67 kHz DC bandwidth. Signals were recorded with a 16-bit digitizer at 250 MS/s and 125 MHz bandwidth. Background contributions from the glass, air, and beam stop were treated as approximately constant and removed by subtracting the average background from the raw signal. The body contribution was modeled as a Gaussian-shaped signal due to the Gaussian spatial profile of the laser beam, while the wing contribution was treated as a periodic modulation caused by changing wing projected area. Local minima in the total backscattered signal, where wing contribution was minimal, were interpolated to estimate the body backscattered power over the full transit. Wing backscattered power was then obtained by subtracting the estimated body power from the total signal. A Fourier transform of the wing signal was used to extract the fundamental wing-beat frequency and harmonics. A Naive Bayes classifier using only wing-beat frequency as the predictor variable was then applied to classify mosquitoes by gender and by species/gender. This method is directly related to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates that near-infrared backscattered time signals from small flying insects can be processed to extract wing-beat frequency and body/wing signal components, which are the same types of physical features proposed for clustering and biodiversity estimation in the field sensor study.
[results]
A total of 427 valid mosquito transit events were recorded across three mosquito groups: Aedes albopictus, Aedes aegypti, and an unidentified Culex genus, with both males and females included. All extracted wing-beat frequencies fell between 200 and 900 Hz. Transit times varied from a few milliseconds to 1300 ms, with an average transit time of 127 ms. For gender classification using wing-beat frequency alone, the Naive Bayes classifier achieved 95.8% accuracy for the male class, 97.6% accuracy for the female class, and 96.5% overall accuracy. For the more complex six-class species/gender classification, class accuracies ranged from 36.2% to 88.6%, and the overall accuracy was 62.3%. The lower six-class accuracy was attributed to greater overlap among the wing-beat frequency distributions as the number of classes increased. No simultaneous multi-insect events were observed among the 427 measurements, although the authors noted that such events would need to be flagged in future work.
[context]
The experiment was conducted in a laboratory optical tube rather than in an autonomous field deployment. The mosquitoes were small, measuring between 3 and 6 mm, and were studied shortly after hatching, less than 7 days old, with measurements continuing until natural death, generally around 15 to 20 days after hatching. Aedes mosquitoes were reared in a university entomology facility, while Culex mosquitoes were collected in the field. Mosquitoes were introduced into the enclosure in batches of ten individuals of the same gender and species. The 1320 nm wavelength was selected to provide measurable backscatter while remaining outside the visual sensitivity range of the studied mosquitoes. The system relied on a high-power continuous-wave laser, a parabolic gold mirror, an InGaAs detector, and a high-speed digitizer. This context is relevant to the proposed method because it shows that wing-beat frequency and body/wing backscattering features can be extracted from near-infrared optical signals under controlled conditions, supporting the feasibility of the proposed feature extraction step. However, the paper does not test the proposed autonomous field sensors, 810 nm and 970 nm LEDs, 5 to 70 L measurement volumes, DBSCAN clustering, or correlation with Malaise traps and sweep nets. It therefore provides supporting evidence for optical feature extraction, but not direct validation of the proposed field-scale biodiversity metric.
[end related work 1]

