[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f)
- venue: Applied physics B
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper used an entomological bistatic optical sensor system to record near-infrared extinction signals from flying insects. A 5 mW continuous-wave 980 nm laser diode was expanded to a 72 mm beam and propagated horizontally over 36 m of mowed vegetation. At the receiving end, a converging lens, a 950-1000 nm bandpass filter, and a 90 kHz silicon photodiode with a 3.6 by 3.6 mm active area recorded the received optical signal at 30,517 Hz using a 16-bit digitizer. Insect passages were identified as short-duration signal drops, typically 20 ms to 1 s, with a Gaussian-like envelope and periodic amplitude drops caused by wingbeats. Raw signals were processed with a 10-900 Hz bandpass filter, and a detection threshold was calculated as a 5 s sliding mean plus 2.5 times the 5 s sliding standard deviation. Candidate events were retained when the signal exceeded this threshold for more than 0.1 ms. Harmonic-series detection in the frequency spectrum was then used to confirm insect signals and estimate wingbeat frequency. For mass estimation, the insect body extinction cross section was related to mass through a fitted coefficient, with separate wet and dry mass relations estimated using Levenberg-Marquardt fitting and evaluated against masses measured on a 0.1 mg scale. This method is related to the proposed Kansas study because both use near-infrared optical sensing to automate detection of flying insects and extract wingbeat-related signal features, although the paper focuses on mass and biomass estimation rather than DBSCAN clustering for species richness.
[results]
Mass estimation from body extinction cross section produced a worst-case dry-mass relative error of 113% for mosquitoes, corresponding to an absolute overestimate of about 1 mg. The best dry-mass prediction was for bumblebees, with a 3.2% relative difference and an average overestimate of 2.4 mg. The paper also reports fitted wet and dry mass-versus-extinction relations, but the provided excerpt does not give their numerical coefficients or statistical significance values.
[context]
The sensor operated for 148 consecutive days from July 13 to December 7, 2021, with downtime below 6.5%, in a semi-urban 40 by 10 m field in Secaucus, New Jersey, bordered by about 1 ha of woodlot. The laser beam was positioned about 20 cm above vegetation that was mowed every other month. The emitter and receiver were protected by tents, the system consumed about 150 W of power from a nearby building, and operation was monitored using a co-located weather station and 4G cloud feedback. Laboratory mass validation included insect groups such as mosquitoes, house flies, mason bees, yellowjackets, and four bumblebees, with masses measured to 0.1 mg precision. The probe volume was approximately the laser beam volume at the intersection with the receiver field of view. This context is relevant to the proposed method because it demonstrates long-term field operation of a near-infrared insect optical sensor and the feasibility of extracting wingbeat and body-related signal features, although it does not test DBSCAN clustering or correlation with Malaise traps and sweep nets.
[end related work 2]

