- in_text_citation: [(Genoud et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5fcd98fb07a48b9ae4a670d6f5ae843ecfdbcd8b)
- venue: Applied physics B
- citation count: 71
[method]
The paper used a continuous-wave 1320 nm infrared laser backscatter system to record time-resolved optical signals from mosquitoes crossing a collimated beam. The detected signal was background-subtracted and modeled as the sum of body and wing backscattered power. Because the body contribution changes slowly and follows the Gaussian spatial profile of the beam, while the wing contribution oscillates rapidly with wing orientation, local minima of the total signal were interpolated to estimate the body backscattered power over the transit. The wing contribution was then obtained by subtracting the interpolated body component from the total signal. A Fourier transform of the wing-only signal was used to extract the fundamental wing-beat frequency and its harmonics. This method is directly related to the proposed research method because it demonstrates extraction of wing-beat frequency from optical backscatter and a body/wing signal separation that is conceptually analogous to deriving a body-to-wing ratio, although the paper did not explicitly compute a body-to-wing ratio feature or use it for DBSCAN clustering.
[results]
From 427 mosquito transit events across three species and both sexes, all extracted wing-beat frequencies fell between 200 and 900 Hz. A Naive Bayes classifier using only wing-beat frequency achieved 95.8% accuracy for males, 97.6% accuracy for females, and 96.5% overall accuracy for gender classification. For a six-class species/gender classification, overall accuracy was 62.3%, with class accuracies ranging from 36.2% to 88.6%, reflecting overlap among the wing-beat frequency distributions of the different species and sexes. No simultaneous multi-mosquito transits were observed among the 427 recorded events.
[context]
Measurements were performed in a controlled Plexiglas tube 1.25 m long and 12.5 cm in diameter, using a 3.6 W 1320 nm laser producing a 2 cm FWHM beam, an off-axis parabolic gold mirror, and an InGaAs amplified photodetector with 67 kHz DC bandwidth. Signals were recorded with a 16-bit digitizer at 250 MS/s and 125 MHz bandwidth. The mosquitoes were 3 to 6 mm in size, less than 7 days old, and included Aedes albopictus, Aedes aegypti, and Culex genus specimens of both sexes. Mosquitoes were introduced in batches of ten, and transit times averaged 127 ms but ranged from a few milliseconds to 1300 ms. This controlled mosquito study differs from the proposed research plan, which involves field deployment of 810 nm and 970 nm LED sensors in agricultural sites and uses wing-beat frequency and body-to-wing ratio for DBSCAN-based richness estimation, but it provides evidence that wing-beat frequency and body/wing signal components can be recovered from near-infrared backscatter.
[end related work 1]

