[method]
The paper implemented a multisensor proof-of-concept system that captured insect images and wingbeat signals as insects passed through a small arena. The camera subsystem used diffuse PMMA illumination, two light barriers, a 500-microsecond LED flash, a 23.5-millisecond exposure, a 10-megapixel teleobjective HQ camera, an H264 video ring buffer, and automatic extraction of three brightness-selected PNG frames. Wingbeat signals were acquired by an optoacoustic sensor. A prediction system was implemented for three input modes: camera only, wingbeat only, and fused camera plus wingbeat. For camera-only classification, a MobileNet convolutional network was trained on the extracted images. For wingbeat-only classification, because the dataset was too small for the CNN approach, a linear support vector machine was used. This method is related to the proposed method because it uses wingbeat-derived signals and morphological visual features as biologically meaningful insect traits for classification, analogous to extracting wing-beat frequency and body-to-wing ratio from optical backscatter for clustering; however, it does not use optical backscatter and does not explicitly compute body-to-wing ratio.
[results]
Camera-only MobileNet classification achieved an overall test-set accuracy of 0.92. Wingbeat-only linear support vector machine classification achieved an accuracy of 0.68. No combined camera-plus-wingbeat accuracy was reported. The wingbeat CNN approach was not evaluated because the available dataset was too small.
[context]
The study used a small multisensor dataset and a simple neural network architecture as a proof of concept. Insects entered from a user-definable trap, passed through a 60 x 45 x 20 mm camera arena, then passed the wingbeat sensor and left the system alive. The camera achieved approximately 10-micrometer resolution at f/8, which was stated to be sufficient for resolving wing vein segments and single hairs. A Raspberry Pi 4 minicomputer controlled the sensors, processed data, stored information on an SD card, and ran a Python-based Bokeh application. No dataset size, species count, statistical significance, DBSCAN clustering, biodiversity metrics, or comparison with conventional sampling methods were reported.
