- in_text_citation: [(Rydhmer, and Selvan, 2021-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/18bfc290cbfd1e6837a5dbe4a72140b3ff96bae3)
- venue: Ecological Informatics
- citation count: 12
[method]
The paper clustered optical insect recordings by first learning a two-dimensional latent representation with a dynamic beta-VAE and then applying K-means to the latent test set. Inputs were 193-bin log-transformed, max-normalized average Welch power spectra derived from near-infrared backscatter at 808 and 975 nm, after a CNN removed non-insect recordings. The encoder had 9 fully connected ReLU layers and the decoder 10 fully connected layers with sigmoid output; the model used a 2D bottleneck, Adam with learning rate 1e-3, batch size 256, 5000 epochs, and PyTorch on an Nvidia GTX 1050. Dynamic beta was updated every 5 epochs using historical minima of reconstruction and regularization losses, with tuned parameters a=0.2, b=0.05, w1=w2=1.2, w3=0.9, w4=1.1. A semi-supervised variant added a clustering loss for 8 labelled species, with gamma cycled between 0.01 and 0.2 every 100 epochs in the parameter section. K-means K was selected from 5 to 50 by maximum average silhouette score. Baselines were PCA, sigmoid Kernel-PCA, complete-linkage HCA, and a conventional VAE. This relates to the proposed DBSCAN plan because both use optical insect signals to form species-like clusters, but the paper uses a learned VAE latent space and K-means rather than DBSCAN and evaluates cluster quality against labelled species rather than correlating cluster count with conventional richness.
[results]
The dynamic beta-VAE reported higher ARI and AMI than the PCA, Kernel-PCA, HCA, and conventional VAE baselines, and the semi-supervised version improved further over the unsupervised version. HCA had the lowest ARI, Kernel-PCA did not show a large improvement over PCA, and the nonadaptive VAE showed large run-to-run variation with frequent collapse, while the dynamic version kept beta below 1 for most of training. The provided excerpt does not include the numeric ARI/AMI values, exact selected K, or statistical significance, so the magnitude of improvement cannot be quantified from the text alone.
[context]
Experiments used 40,000 unlabelled field recordings from March to November 2020 in the Öresund region, 6,000 labelled recordings from 12 species groups (500 per group), with 8 species used in the semi-supervised clustering loss and 4 held out as labelled test species. A 3,000-recording unlabelled test set was clustered after each of 5 random training/test splits. The sensor illuminated a 20 L volume at 1 m, sampled at 20 kHz with 0-5 kHz bandwidth, and the spectra were reduced to 193 bins between 0 and 2 kHz. The 2D latent dimension was chosen for visualization, and K was constrained to 5-50 based on the prior that the data contained at least 5 species. The model-parameter section reports gamma cycling between 0.01 and 0.2, whereas the discussion reports 0.01 and 2, a discrepancy relevant to reproducibility. The setting differs from the proposed Kansas agricultural study: it uses spectral WBF features rather than explicit WBF and body-to-wing ratio, evaluates cluster agreement with four labelled species, and does not use DBSCAN or compare cluster counts to Malaise trap or sweep-net richness.
[end related work 1]

