- in_text_citation: [(Armando et al., 2022-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1412fadfe026f74fe7b94bd07c23d78bbad09625)
- venue: IOP Conference Series: Earth and Environment
- citation count: 2
[method]
The paper used conventional arthropod sampling and taxonomic identification in three oil palm plantation fields. In each 100 m by 100 m field, five 100 m aisles spaced 9.4 m apart were sampled, with 20 intentionally selected sample plants per field, rotated by an interval of two plants between observations. Arthropods were collected with pitfall traps for ground-surface activity, swing nets for flying and canopy-associated insects, and direct observation of the plant canopy. Specimens were identified in a laboratory to taxonomic units, mainly orders and families, with some genera noted. This is related to the proposed conventional component because it uses physical collection and taxonomic identification to generate biodiversity estimates, but it differs from the proposed method by reporting family-level richness and a diversity index rather than species or morphospecies richness, Shannon, and Simpson indices, and by using pitfall traps and direct observation rather than Malaise traps and sweep nets.
[results]
Across the three fields, the conventional sampling recovered 81 arthropod families in total. Pitfall traps yielded 37 families and 2010 individuals, swing nets yielded 57 families and 315 individuals, and direct observation yielded 30 families and 210 individuals. Swing net sampling had the highest reported arthropod diversity index. Insects were the most abundant class, followed by Arachnida and Diplopoda. Predatory arthropods were the largest ecological role group, dominated by Formicidae; Acrididae was the dominant herbivorous family; Isotomidae was the dominant detritivore; and parasitoids were the least abundant role group, with Braconidae, Eulophidae, and Ichneumonidae reported as dominant parasitoid families.
[context]
The study was conducted in 2015 at an oil palm plantation in Cigudeg, Bogor Regency, West Java, Indonesia. Three fields represented different habitat conditions: a relatively clean field, a field with natural vegetation, and a field with legume cover crops, including Mucuna bracteata. Fields I and II were adjacent to a highway and residential areas and were 10 years old; field III was adjacent to rice fields and residential areas and was 5 years old; all were considered mature plantations. The experimental units were 100 m by 100 m fields with five 100 m aisles and 9.4 m aisle spacing. The method provides context for conventional biodiversity assessment in an agricultural system, but it is not directly comparable to the proposed research plan because it lacks optical sensor data, species-level or morphospecies-level identification, voucher specimen reporting, and the specified Shannon and Simpson metrics.
[end related work 2]



