- in_text_citation: [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f)
- venue: Applied physics B
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper implemented a continuous field optical sensor, eBoss, to detect flying insects and estimate mass. A 980 nm, 5 mW continuous laser diode was expanded to a 72 mm beam and sent horizontally 36 m through a probe volume about 20 cm above mowed vegetation. A receiver lens, 950-1000 nm bandpass filter, and amplified silicon photodiode recorded the attenuated optical signal at 30,517 Hz with a 16-bit digitizer over a 5 V range. A desktop computer and 4G LTE router stored data and sent operation feedback to the cloud. Raw voltage time series were filtered with a 10-900 Hz band-pass filter; potential events were flagged when extinction exceeded a sliding average plus 2.5 times the sliding standard deviation over 5 s for more than 0.1 ms. Insect events were confirmed by detecting harmonic series associated with wingbeat periodicity, which also allowed separation of wing and body signal contributions. Extinction cross sections were derived from the signal drops and used in a fitted mass-extinction relation, estimated with a Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm, to predict wet and dry insect mass. Evaluation used laboratory specimens with known wet and dry masses measured on a 0.1 mg precision scale. This is related to the proposed method because both use continuous near-infrared optical sensing in fields to capture flying-insect transits and extract wingbeat-related features; the paper provides a field-tested event-detection and mass-estimation pipeline, while the proposed plan uses 810/970 nm LED backscatter sensors, WBF and body-to-wing ratio, DBSCAN clustering as a richness proxy, and comparison with Malaise traps and sweep nets.
[results]
In the laboratory mass-estimation test, the extinction-cross-section model produced small absolute errors for small insects but large relative errors for very small dry masses. The worst percent error was 113% for dry mosquitoes, but the absolute error was only 1 mg, indicating an overestimate of about 1 mg. The best percent error was 3.2% for dry bumblebees, with an average overestimate of 2.4 mg. The field campaign ran for 148 consecutive days with downtime below 6.5%, demonstrating sustained operation, but the provided excerpt does not report quantitative seasonal or daily biomass density values, species richness estimates, or direct correlations with conventional sampling methods.
[context]
The sensor was deployed from July 13 to December 7, 2021 in a 40 x 10 m semi-urban field in Secaucus, New Jersey, bordered by a woodlot, with a co-located weather station. The laser path was about 20 cm above vegetation that was mowed every other month; the emitter and receiver were protected by tents, and the system consumed about 150 W from a nearby building, so it was not fully autonomous. The detector had a 90 kHz bandwidth and a 3.6 x 3.6 mm active area, and the main noise sources were the acquisition card, unwanted infrared sources, and mechanical vibration. Laboratory mass estimation used known insect masses measured with a 0.1 mg precision scale across groups including mosquitoes, house flies, mason bees, yellowjackets, and bumblebees, with four bumblebee specimens noted. This context is relevant to the proposed 3-day autonomous deployments 50 m from Kansas agricultural field margins because it shows that continuous optical insect detection and wingbeat-based classification are feasible in the field, but the paper's 36 m bistatic 980 nm laser geometry, mass/biomass objective, and non-agricultural semi-urban setting differ from the proposed monostatic LED backscatter, 810/970 nm, DBSCAN richness proxy, and 20-site crop comparison.
[end related work 3]

Processing method: Context: To test whether optical near-infrared insect sensors can automate measurement of an insect biodiversity metric. Method: We will deploy autonomous near-infrared sensors alongside conventional sampling methods (Malaise traps and sweep nets) in agricultural fields across Kansas, USA.
