- in_text_citation: [(Kiskin et al., 2018-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2619b9ae34fcc27fa7c84c43cc60aec877fc540f)
- venue: Neural computing & applications (Print)
- citation count: 79
[method]
The paper used a wavelet-transform-based convolutional neural network to classify short audio intervals as mosquito present or absent. The input was a single-channel wavelet scalogram with 256 frequency-like scales, and the CNN used a single convolutional layer, ReLU activations, dropout, and a fully connected layer producing two output classes. The model was trained on 0.1 s label intervals from mosquito recordings, with hyperparameters selected by 10-fold cross-validation to maximize F1 score. A traditional SVM baseline was also evaluated using hand-designed audio features, including STFT slices, MFCCs, entropy, energy entropy, spectral entropy, flux, roll-off, spread, centroid, and zero crossing rate, with feature selection by recursive feature elimination. This relates to the proposed research method because both use a trained classifier to separate insect-related recordings from noise before downstream analysis, although the paper uses acoustic mosquito wingbeat recordings rather than optical near-infrared time signals and does not extract wing-beat frequency or body-to-wing ratio.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical classification metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, or F1 values. It reports qualitatively that wavelet-based training outperformed conventional STFT-based training, that the SVM was the best-performing traditional classifier among random forests, naive Bayes, and SVMs, and that the model showed strong transfer to held-out data without hyperparameter re-tuning. Cross-validated parameter search identified a window width of 10 as optimal for the CNN and a window width of 1 as optimal for the SVM. For the traditional classifiers, recursive feature elimination with 27 selected features outperformed all principal component analysis reductions.
[context]
The mosquito dataset consisted of 57 recordings collected in January 2016 from culture cages containing male and female Culex quinquefasciatus. Recordings were made with inexpensive smartphone microphones and sampled at 8 kHz because higher-frequency harmonics were not reliably audible in noisy conditions. Labels were two-class presence or absence at 0.1 s granularity, created by four human researchers using Audacity, with one carefully made label set treated as a gold standard. The data were split 50% training and 50% held out, and the training portion was further split into 10 folds for cross-validation, producing approximately 3,000 to 30,000 training samples depending on window width. Computational resources are not specified in the excerpt. The context is relevant to the proposed optical sensor study as an example of automatic insect-versus-noise filtering, but it is not directly transferable because it addresses audio detection of mosquitoes rather than optical detection of general insect wingbeats and body-to-wing ratios.
[end related work 1]

