- in_text_citation: [(Rydhmer, and Selvan, 2021-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/18bfc290cbfd1e6837a5dbe4a72140b3ff96bae3)
- venue: Ecological Informatics
- citation count: 12
[method]
The paper trained a dynamic beta-VAE to learn a two-dimensional latent representation from 193-dimensional log-transformed, max-normalized Welch power spectra of insect wing-beat signals. The encoder had 9 fully connected ReLU layers and predicted the mean and variance of the approximate posterior; the decoder had 10 fully connected layers with ReLU activations except a final sigmoid layer. The standard beta-VAE objective was modified so that beta was adapted every 5 epochs by comparing reconstruction and regularization losses to their historical minima; if either loss increased above a threshold relative to its historical minimum, beta was adjusted upward or downward. A semi-supervised variant added a clustering loss for 8 labelled species groups, with the clustering-loss scaling gamma cycled between 0.01 and 0.2 every 100 epochs. After training, latent embeddings of an unlabelled test set were clustered with K-means, and the number of clusters K was selected from 5 to 50 by maximum average silhouette score. Baselines were PCA, sigmoid Kernel-PCA, complete-linkage hierarchical clustering, and a conventional non-adaptive VAE. This method is related to the proposed research plan because both use optical near-infrared insect recordings and WBF-derived spectral features to form species-like clusters, but the paper uses a learned VAE latent space and K-means rather than DBSCAN, and it evaluates clusters against labelled species with ARI and AMI rather than tuning DBSCAN parameters to maximize Spearman correlation with conventional biodiversity metrics.
[results]
The supplied excerpt reports that the dynamic beta-VAE achieved higher ARI and AMI than PCA, Kernel-PCA, hierarchical clustering, and the conventional VAE, and that semi-supervised training further improved clustering over the unsupervised dynamic beta-VAE. Hierarchical clustering had the lowest ARI, Kernel-PCA did not show a drastic improvement over PCA, and the non-adaptive VAE showed large performance variation and frequent collapse. The paper states that clusters were more compact and less overlapping with semi-supervision, but it does not provide numeric ARI/AMI values, exact selected K values, or statistical significance tests in the supplied text.
[context]
Experiments used 40,000 unlabelled field recordings from March to November 2020 in the Oresund region, southern Scandinavia, collected with a FaunaPhotonics instrument using 808 nm and 975 nm illumination, a 20 L measurement volume within 1 m, 20 kHz sampling, and 0-5 kHz bandwidth; signals were filtered by a CNN and reduced to 193 frequency bins between 0 and 2 kHz. An additional 6,000 labelled recordings from 12 closed-cage species groups in Copenhagen were used, with 500 recordings per group; 8 species groups were used in the semi-supervised clustering loss and 4 held out for testing. A random subset of 3,000 recordings served as the test set, and all models were run five times on random splits. Models were implemented in PyTorch, trained for 5,000 epochs with Adam at learning rate 1e-3, batch size 256, on an Nvidia GTX 1050 with 4 GB memory. Dynamic beta parameters were a=0.2, b=0.05, w1=w2=1.2, w3=0.9, w4=1.1, tuned on an independent dataset from a different location with similar instrumentation. The bottleneck was limited to 2 dimensions for visualization, and the cluster range 5-50 incorporated a prior that the data contained at least 5 species.
[end related work 1]

