ure free-flying WBF or BWR. Taken as an iterative sequence, the 2018 laboratory proof, the 2019 commercial autonomy evidence, and the 2023 field wingbeat detection collectively support the proposed continuous near-infrared field monitoring, but only partially support the proposed 50 m, 810/970 nm, DBSCAN-based richness workflow.",
  "contradictions": "The most important contradiction is that none of the related works validates the proposed DBSCAN cluster-count proxy for species richness or its correlation with Malaise traps and sweep nets. [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f) reported sustained field detection and mass estimation but no species richness, Shannon or Simpson indices, or Spearman correlations with conventional sampling; its mass model had large relative errors for small insects, 113% for dry mosquitoes with an absolute error of 1 mg, and smaller errors for larger insects, 3.2% for dry bumblebees with an average overestimate of 2.4 mg, indicating that optical signal features may not map cleanly to taxonomic richness. The next strongest contradiction is that the field evidence is not directly transferable to the proposed deployment: [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f) used a 36 m bistatic 980 nm laser, 20 cm above mowed vegetation in a semi-urban field, consumed about 150 W from a building, and was not fully autonomous, whereas the plan requires an autonomous sensor 50 m from Kansas crop field margins for two 3-day periods in June and July. The third major contradiction is that the strongest WBF classification evidence is laboratory and limited to mosquitoes: [(Genoud et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5fcd98fb07a48b9ae4a670d6f5ae843ecfdbcd8b) used a controlled 1.25 m tube, 427 transits, no simultaneous multi-insect events, and six-class species/gender accuracy only 62.3% with class accuracies 36.2-88.6%, showing substantial overlap among WBF distributions and no evidence that cluster counts will scale to diverse agricultural insect communities. The weakest contradiction is [(Parsons et al., 2019-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0fee975fcf2bd48492d8d4b47f5761bc0201928d), which provides no quantitative detection, classification, or biodiversity performance and is trap-based rather than free-flying, so it cannot support the proposed optical richness metric.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to add an explicit validation layer for the DBSCAN richness proxy before treating cluster count as a biodiversity metric: use the 20-site, June and July design to compare DBSCAN cluster counts with Malaise and sweep-net species richness, Shannon, and Simpson indices, tune epsilon and min_samples on the 30% optimization set, evaluate on the 70% test set, report Spearman correlations with confidence intervals and statistical significance, and test cluster stability across the two 3-day periods, crops, and fields. Second, adopt and adapt the event-detection pipeline from [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f), including 10-900 Hz band-pass filtering, sliding-average plus 2.5 standard-deviation extinction threshold, harmonic wingbeat confirmation, and body/wing separation, while recalibrating for 810/970 nm LED backscatter, 50 m monostatic geometry, and 5-70 L measurement volumes; include multi-insect flags because [(Genoud et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5fcd98fb07a48b9ae4a670d6f5ae843ecfdbcd8b) observed no simultaneous transits and noted multiple Fourier peaks could flag them. Third, ensure true autonomy and operational reliability for two 3-day deployments by using solar/lithium power, LTE/cloud upload, GPS, weather logging, and downtime monitoring, as in [(Parsons et al., 2019-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0fee975fcf2bd48492d8d4b47f5761bc0201928d) and [(Genoud et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d99be82ff97b0e19d5678704ae7d6d3a41a4387f), and pre-test the 50 m field-margin placement against the 36 m 980 nm field geometry. Fourth, address the low multi-class WBF classification evidence from [(Genoud et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5fcd98fb07a48b9ae4a670d6f5ae843ecfdbcd8b) by not relying on WBF alone for species-level inference; incorporate BWR, harmonics, amplitude, and temporal features, and discard or flag recordings without clear WBF/BWR as planned. Finally, strengthen statistical inference by reporting ANOVA and TukeyHSD for month, crop, and field, controlling for multiple comparisons, and using voucher-identified conventional samples from Malaise traps and sweep nets to calibrate the optical proxy.",
